# What do people in your country often do while waiting?
I think people do different things while waiting, depending on their age and personality. Older people tend to read newspapers, magazines, or books to pass the time, especially in places like hospitals or bus stations. Some of them also enjoy chatting with people nearby.

Younger people, on the other hand, are more likely to use their smartphones. For example, they may scroll through social media, watch videos, play games, or text their friends while waiting. Personally, I usually listen to music or browse the internet because it helps me feel less bored and makes the waiting time pass more quickly.

# Why do some people like a slow-paced life?
I think many people are attracted to a slow-paced lifestyle because it helps them feel less stressed and enjoy a better work-life balance. Nowadays, modern life is often very fast-paced and competitive, so people can easily feel overwhelmed by work, studies, and social pressure.

By slowing down, people usually have more time to relax, take care of their physical and mental health, and strengthen their relationships with family and friends. In addition, a slower lifestyle allows people to appreciate small moments in life instead of constantly rushing from one task to another. That’s why many people see slow living as a way to improve their overall quality of life.

# Is being patient good for people? Why?
Yes, I think patience is definitely an important quality because many things in life take time and effort to achieve. People often face challenges, setbacks, or pressure in their studies, work, and personal lives, so being patient helps them stay calm and avoid giving up too quickly.

I also think patience contributes to emotional stability because patient people are usually better at handling stress and difficult situations. In many cases, success requires long-term effort rather than immediate results.

However, I believe there should be a balance. If someone is too patient or passive, they may miss opportunities or hesitate to take action when necessary. So in my opinion, patience is valuable, but people should also know when to act decisively.

# Are people less patient now than people in the past? Why?
I think, in some ways, people today are less patient than people in the past. Modern life is much faster and more competitive, and technology allows people to get things almost instantly, such as information, entertainment, or online shopping. As a result, many people have become used to immediate results and may feel frustrated when things take too long.

On the other hand, I also believe people nowadays can be quite patient and resilient when it comes to long-term goals like education or career development. Many people are willing to work under pressure for years in order to achieve success or improve their quality of life. So overall, I think people may be less patient in daily situations, but still capable of showing patience in important aspects of life.

# Why do children lack patience?
I think children often lack patience because they are still developing emotionally and mentally. At a young age, they usually want immediate satisfaction and may find it difficult to control their emotions when things do not go the way they expect.

In addition, children generally have less life experience and weaker problem-solving skills compared to adults, so they can become frustrated more easily when facing challenges or difficult tasks. I also think modern technology plays a role because many children are now used to getting instant entertainment from smartphones or the internet, which may make them less willing to wait.

# How can people become more patient?
I think people can become more patient by learning how to manage their emotions and reduce stress. For example, activities like deep breathing, meditation, or exercise can help people stay calm and react less impulsively in difficult situations.

Another useful way is to practice gratitude and develop a more positive mindset. For instance, some people write down things they are thankful for every day, which helps them focus less on frustration and more on appreciation. I also believe patience improves naturally with experience because people gradually learn that many worthwhile things in life require time and effort.
